<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Arabia and the Gulf</p><h1 class="st-title">Emirate of Nejd</h1><p class="st-meta">1824 CE – 1891 CE · capital Riyadh</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The second Saudi state, which revived Al Saud rule from Riyadh after the destruction of Diriyah, until the Rashidis overthrew it.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Six years after the Ottoman-Egyptian destruction of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/emirate-of-diriyah">Emirate of Diriyah</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turki-ibn-abdullah">Turki ibn Abdullah</a>, a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-saud">Muhammad ibn Saud</a>, drove the Egyptian garrison from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riyadh">Riyadh</a> in 1824 and made it his capital. He restored Saudi rule over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najd">Najd</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-ahsa-oasis">al-Hasa</a> and renewed the alliance with the Wahhabi scholars.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> was smaller than its predecessor and avoided war with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, leaving the holy cities alone. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faisal-ibn-turki-al-saud">Faisal ibn Turki</a>, who ruled twice, consolidated it; between his reigns <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riyadh">Riyadh</a> again from 1838 to 1843. The state taxed oasis farmers and tribes and drew on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Gulf</a> trade through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-ahsa-oasis">al-Hasa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qatif">Qatif</a>. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/emirate-of-jabal-shammar">Emirate of Jabal Shammar</a> in the north was at first a loyal ally.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After Faisal died in 1865, his sons Abdullah and Saud fought a long civil war, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-ahsa-oasis">al-Hasa</a> in 1871. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ibn-rashid">Muhammad ibn Rashid</a> of Ha'il took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riyadh">Riyadh</a> in 1887 and defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-saud">Al Saud</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mulayda">Mulayda</a> in 1891; the family fled to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sheikhdom-of-kuwait">Sheikhdom of Kuwait</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/emirate-of-nejd.jpg" alt="Emirate of Nejd" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: David First · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_First_and_Second_Saudi_State_(1744-1891).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1824 CE – 1891 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Riyadh</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Turki ibn Abdullah, Faisal ibn Turki, Abdullah ibn Faisal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam (Wahhabi)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Second Saudi State, Emirate of Riyadh (1824-1891)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1858&amp;focus=emirate-of-nejd" aria-label="Emirate of Nejd on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5921 1271 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Emirate of Nejd lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6241 1527L6237 1523M6241 1527L5709 970M6241 1527L6095 1425M6241 1527L6241 1527"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6237 1523h0M5709 970h0M6095 1425h0M6241 1527h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6241 1527h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6241 1527h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Riyadh. <a href="/atlas?year=1858&amp;focus=emirate-of-nejd">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
